WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:05.000
Some movie scenes are so hilarious, exciting,
or romantic that we can go back to watch them

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.420
again and again and never get tired of them.

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:12.440
Then there are the other ones: the unsettling
moments and creepy scenes that stick with

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.740
us even if we only watched them once.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:19.240
Here are just a few of the uncomfortable movie
scenes that we'll never, ever watch again.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:25.760
In The Nice Guys, Holland March is the worst
detective in Los Angeles, maybe even the world.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:28.500
"It's just Fred's never been gone this long
before."

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:32.420
"How long has he been missing?"

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.040
"Since the funeral."

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:40.600
Played by a hilarious Ryan Gosling, March
is a bumbling alcoholic who's hired to find

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.120
a missing adult film star.

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:47.680
His quest is filled with all sorts of injuries,
both to his pride and his well-being, but

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:51.240
the most painful moment comes when he accidentally
slits his wrist.

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:56.220
At one point, March tries the old P.I. trick
of just wrapping up his hand, punching through

00:00:56.229 --> 00:00:59.940
a small pane of glass, and reaching inside
to unlock the door.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.820
But March is no action hero.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.500
Needless to say, he immediately regrets it.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:09.600
Granted, the scene is played for laughs, and
Gosling does an amazing job selling the comedy...

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:10.600
"A lot of blood…

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:11.160
That's a…

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:12.160
That’s a lot of blood."

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:18.240
… but, seeing (and hearing) all that blood
leaves us feeling queasy, and that definitely

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:19.760
isn't nice, guys.

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:25.000
With a title like Creep, it should come as
no surprise that this horror flick is one

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:26.500
uncomfortable experience.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:31.530
The film follows an unlucky cameraman named
Aaron who's hired to make a video for a weirdo

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:37.160
named Josef, who claims he's dying of cancer
and wants to make a film for his unborn child.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:42.960
But this seemingly sweet idea gets freakier
and freakier as Josef abandons Aaron in the

00:01:42.969 --> 00:01:48.119
woods, tells him a disturbing tale about his
marriage, and performs a song-and-dance number

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.100
while wearing a wolf mask.

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:52.700
"At the end of the day, it’s gonna be normal,
so…"

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:53.880
"Oh, okay."

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:58.400
"Trust me, that’s not gonna be anything
weird at all."

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.020
But all that comes after Josef’s first bit
of creepiness.

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:06.240
Early in the film, he tells Aaron to follow
him into the bathroom for "tubby time."

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:09.380
When Josef was a little kid, he would take
baths with his dad.

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:14.100
Since he won't be around to share this with
his son, Josef wants to recreate that experience

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:14.700
on film.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.820
And yeah, watching from Aaron’s perspective
as Josef strips down and starts playing with

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:24.940
an imaginary baby in the bathtub is about
as awkward and unsettling as it sounds.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:26.600
Then, it gets weirder.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:32.480
One moment, Josef is playing with his imaginary
kid, and the next, he sinks into a depression,

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.120
starts talking about s, and slides under
water like he plans to drown .

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:41.960
When Aaron leans over the tub to check on
Josef and the madman comes screaming at the

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:47.260
camera, it's quite the jump scare and perfectly
sets the mood for all the creepiness to come.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.020
Steve Carell is no stranger to cringe-worthy
moments…

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:55.560
"Been a lot of fun talk about prison today,
but I am here to scare you straight!

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.900
I AM HERE TO SCARE YOU STRAAAAIGHT!"

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:04.900
... but after leaving The Office, he wound
up in more awkward situations in Foxcatcher.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:10.640
This film, based on a tragic true story, finds
Carell playing John du Pont, an eccentric

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:14.700
millionaire with a fondness for firearms,
cocaine, and combat sports.

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:19.140
Even though he's incredibly rich, du Pont
is still desperate to win the approval of

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:23.840
his mother, a cold woman more fond of her
prize-winning horses than her own son.

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:28.660
Searching for just a tiny bit of praise, du
Pont starts a wrestling team in hopes of winning

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:29.760
some Olympic gold.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:34.060
Even though he's just a novice when it comes
to the sport, he takes control of the class

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:39.040
from coach Dave Schultz, played by Mark Ruffalo,
and starts giving an inspirational speech

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:43.100
to the athletes about representing America
and becoming "winners in life."

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:48.040
Things get especially cringey when he calls
one of the wrestlers onto the mat and demonstrates

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:51.800
some incredibly basic techniques in an attempt
to impress his mom.

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:56.320
Eventually, du Pont winds up on the ground
with a guy on his back, and that's when his

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:58.700
mom leaves with a disapproving scowl.

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:03.700
The look on du Pont's face is heartbreaking,
and even though he's a manipulative psycho,

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:08.320
watching this man humiliate himself will make
you wrestle with some complicated feelings.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:12.860
"I don’t like the sport of wrestling, as
you know.

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:17.180
It’s a low sport."

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:23.220
Even by the standards of Quentin Tarantino,
The Hateful Eight is full of mean, disgusting

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:25.540
moments that will make you squirm in your
seat.

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:30.660
In fact, it might have the single grossest
scene that Tarantino has ever put to film:

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:32.360
the poisoned coffee sequence.

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:36.760
The Hateful Eight finds a bunch of killers
trapped inside a cabin as a blizzard rages

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:37.260
outside.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:42.160
Among these shootists and scoundrels, we've
got John Ruth, a bounty hunter handcuffed

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:44.600
to notorious murderer Daisy Domergue.

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:48.900
Ruth knows that Daisy has an inside man in
the cabin who wants to kill him and set his

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:54.440
outlaw boss free, but Ruth never suspects
that mysterious mole would go so far as to

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:55.180
poison his coffee.

00:04:55.460 --> 00:05:00.911
After takes a swig from his mug, he immediately
starts vomiting what we can only call… extra

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:02.020
chunky blood.

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:02.980
"Really?

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:06.520
Nobody got a problem with this?"

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:11.280
That kicks off a chain reaction in the film,
and maybe even the audience, and by the end

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:16.100
of it, Ruth is dead, and we’re looking sideways
at Starbucks for the rest of our lives.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:20.300
"You got anything in here besides coffee that
can help warm us up?"

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:22.280
"The bar is open!

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:23.540
Follow moi."

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.380
Annihilation isn't what you'd call a feel-good
flick.

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:33.940
Directed by Alex Garland, this sci-fi movie
deals with some heavy themes like regret,

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:35.420
infidelity, and depression.

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:40.740
On top of all that, you've got the Shimmer,
an alien electromagnetic field that mixes

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:45.760
and matches the DNA of any living creature
that steps inside its translucent walls.

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:50.400
As a result, we're treated to a nightmare
world where deformed animals scream like human

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:55.880
beings, alligator-sharks grow to enormous
size, and Tessa Thompson turns into a tree.

00:05:56.090 --> 00:06:00.420
But the most horrific image inside the Shimmer
comes when our heroes discover an old video

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:03.380
camera that reveals the fate of an earlier
expedition.

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:08.639
When they play the footage, we see a wild-eyed
soldier played by Oscar Isaac performing some

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:11.060
amateur surgery on one of his comrades.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:16.580
That’s bad enough by itself, but then we
see why: thanks to the Shimmer's gene-splicing

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:21.060
abilities, this guy's intestines have come
to life, and they're oozing around like some

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:21.920
giant worm.

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:27.140
It's a scene that starts off sadistic before
taking a turn into Lovecraft territory, and

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:31.300
no matter how hard you try, you'll never be
able to annihilate this moment from your memory.

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:37.340
The King of Comedy was the fourth collaboration
between Robert De Niro and Martin Scorsese,

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:42.490
and while it's not as well known as Taxi Driver
or Raging Bull, it's definitely the most awkward

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:43.520
movie they've ever made.

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:48.330
A black comedy about the dark side of show
business, the film finds De Niro as Rupert

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:53.331
Pupkin, a wannabe comedian who idolizes late
night talk show host Jerry Langford, played

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:54.340
by Jerry Lewis.

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:58.810
Rupert thinks if he could just get on Jerry's
show and perform his set, then he could make

00:06:58.810 --> 00:06:59.840
it into the big time.

00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:05.180
However, Rupert is a delusional creep who
fantasizes about being Jerry's best friend.

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:10.360
Eventually, the fantasy spills over into reality
when Rupert starts stalking Jerry, and even

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:13.300
shows up at Jerry's mansion, completely uninvited.

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:18.560
Making things worse, Rupert has brought along
a date, who has no idea that her psycho boyfriend

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:20.640
isn't really friends with the talk show host.

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:23.540
"Oh, no, no, we just thought we’d drop in
uninvited for the weekend."

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:30.060
Slowly but surely, Rupert's date realizes
they're not supposed to be in this man's house,

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:34.620
and we can totally feel this poor woman's
embarrassment, but Rupert just keeps on pretending

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:36.020
that he's Jerry's best friend.

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:41.020
The whole scene is shudderingly awkward, and
it feels so genuinely raw and confrontational

00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:43.620
because the actors were improvising all their
lines.

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:49.620
Darren Aronofsky‘s Requiem For a Dream might
be the most traumatic film of all time, and

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:52.480
probably the greatest anti-drug PSA ever made.

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:58.040
Every moment with Ellen Burstyn as Sarah Goldfarb
is just heartbreaking, all building up to

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:03.360
the infamous climax where our four main characters
experience what it must be like to visit hell.

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:07.680
If we had to pick one element of the film
that absolutely forces viewers to look away

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:11.420
in disgust, though, it could be any scene
involving Jared Leto's arm.

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:13.180
"What’s the problem?"

00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:14.840
"It’s my arm!

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:17.000
My arm is killing me!"

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:18.160
"Let’s have a look at it."

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:23.300
Leto plays Harry Goldfarb, a heroin addict
whose intravenous drug use causes a vein in

00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:25.560
one of his arms to become horribly infected.

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:30.360
In one of the saddest moments of the film,
the arm grows so disgusting that it’s amputated

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:35.120
during an operation, but before that awful
moment, we witness a jittery Harry prepare

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:38.280
to shoot up by sticking the needle in his
swollen, black arm.

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:44.350
The injection site is oozing with dark liquid,
but Harry jabs that needle into the vein anyway,

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:47.440
causing moviegoers everywhere to say no to
drugs.

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:53.120
The Square is an award-winning satire of the
contemporary art world, but it also contains

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:56.620
an infamously show-stopping sequence: the
Monkey Man scene.

00:08:57.010 --> 00:09:01.120
It happens at a prestigious dinner party,
where a muscle-bound artist storms into a

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:03.100
room full of wealthy patrons.

00:09:03.309 --> 00:09:07.720
This guy is hunched over, with stilts attached
to his arms, and he's howling like an ape.

00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:12.369
He's a performance artist pretending to be
a chimpanzee, and at first, it seems like

00:09:12.369 --> 00:09:13.260
fun and games.

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:18.319
He waddles around the room, grunts, and amuses
the patrons with his eerily accurate chimp

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:18.820
impression.

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:24.120
But just like real chimps, the artist gets
ultra-aggressive incredibly fast, stalking

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:26.320
around the room and harassing guests.

00:09:26.569 --> 00:09:31.540
The terrified patrons keep perfectly quiet
and still, but things finally go way too far

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:36.410
when he leaps on a table and assaults a female
patron, prompting everyone in the room to

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:37.400
beat him to a pulp.

00:09:37.850 --> 00:09:42.540
This insane performance goes on for around
12 minutes, and perhaps it feels so real because

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:46.820
Notary actually picked on extras he sensed
were nervous whenever he walked by.

00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:50.940
Don’t worry too much, though: the woman
he attacked was played by a stunt person who

00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:53.040
was aware of and ready for the scene.

00:09:53.369 --> 00:09:57.899
The final product is unbearably tense and
amazingly uncomfortable, and just like the

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:02.020
horror-struck patrons, we don't want to revisit
this terrifying experience again.

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:05.160
Tusk is not a great movie.

00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:10.380
It bombed at the box office, it offended critics,
and it features one of Johnny Depp's worst

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:12.220
performances, which is saying something.

00:10:12.550 --> 00:10:17.240
There are, however, a couple of killer scenes,
and perhaps the most horrifically uncomfortable

00:10:17.259 --> 00:10:22.649
moment comes when podcaster Wallace Bryton,
played by Justin Long, realizes he's missing

00:10:22.649 --> 00:10:24.460
a pretty important body part.

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:28.920
After meeting an eccentric adventurer called
Howard Howe while looking for a good story

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:33.920
for his show, Wallace drinks some spiked tea,
and wakes up later in a wheelchair, heavily

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:35.840
anesthetized and minus a leg.

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:40.260
Still under the effects of Howard's spiked
tea, Wallace is freaks out when he sees his

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:44.990
stitched-up stump, and Howard explains that
a doctor had to amputate the limb after Wallace

00:10:44.990 --> 00:10:46.820
was bitten by a brown recluse.

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:51.640
But as tears well up in Wallace's eyes, Howard
is trying to hold back laughter.

00:10:51.980 --> 00:10:55.920
After all, he's the one who cut the leg off
as part of a twisted experiment.

00:10:56.380 --> 00:11:01.300
Tusk is a little tough to watch for a lot
of reasons, but in this case, it’s on purpose.

00:11:02.540 --> 00:11:08.600
From its 2001: A Space Odyssey-style opening
to its fiery finale, everything about 2013’s

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:09.960
Under the Skin is eerie.

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:14.820
Even though this film has dudes getting sucked
out of their skin, however, the most unsettling

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:16.900
moment has got to be that awful beach scene.

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:22.500
Scarlett Johansson plays an extraterrestrial
being who lures men to their deaths, usually

00:11:22.509 --> 00:11:27.221
by driving around in a van, picking up guys
and taking them back to her apartment, where

00:11:27.221 --> 00:11:29.260
they meet a fate worse than death.

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:34.100
In this particular scene, though, Johansson
is hunting for prey at a Scottish beach, where

00:11:34.100 --> 00:11:36.840
she witnesses a woman getting pulled out into
the sea.

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:41.600
She dispassionately watches the woman disappear
into the waves, and she doesn't move to help

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:44.160
when the woman's lover drowns trying to save
her.

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:48.949
In fact, she only reacts after a would-be
hero tries in vain to save the couple and

00:11:48.949 --> 00:11:50.560
is washed ashore unconscious.

00:11:50.989 --> 00:11:56.519
She drags the man to her van, intent on killing
him, and completely ignores the drowned couple’s

00:11:56.520 --> 00:11:59.060
abandoned baby, crying in terror on the beach.

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:03.800
Johansson’s character never feels more inhuman
and predatory than she does here, and her

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:07.220
cruel indifference leaves a sick, cold feeling
in our stomachs.

